icates the message and keeps  everyone in line ideally that's how it  works if you're going to expect that out  of your quarterback  you can't freeze him out you just can't  you can't tell him sit down shut up you  just work here so  i don't know that influence is the right  answer but i definitely think they  should hear him out i think it's smart  to hear out your key players you guys  who you're building the team around and  with quarterbacks becoming more and more  important every year yeah if you've got  a guy that you're going to value for  years into the future why do you not  want to make him a partner in the way  that you're running your football team  because he's the one who's going to be  expected to inspire that guy along with  everyone else to elevate the team above  the sum of the individual parts that's  what a great quarterback can do so i  think you empower your quarterback to  have a voice and if you disagree with  what he's saying  you have the respect for him that he's  going to understand it  and you talk to him like an adult and  you tell him why you're going to go in a  different direction it's not that  complicated  yeah you answered my question mike and i  was going to ask if conor murray had  earned that one pro bowl that frankly  should have gone to tom brady last  season no playoff appearances but you  answered it because yes they do value  him as their franchise quarterback  that's how they see him as their  franchise quarterback i always say this  i would think he would have more say if  they're looking on the off offensive  side of the ball i just remember troy  aikman going and working out prospects  one year throwing with attack which  couldn't do that this year but throwing  with the tight ends and the receivers  and players that they were interested in  i know he did that and so then they got  his feedback and and that's perfectly  acceptable if if something like that  happens and you know kyle murray was in  college recently and 